  <h1>Common Dog Bite Claim Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The dog never bit anyone before, so the owner isn&rsquo;t liable.&rdquo;</h2><p>In strict liability states, prior history is irrelevant &mdash; the owner is liable for the first bite. In one-bite rule states, lack of prior incidents does make the case harder, but negligence theories (unleashed dog, failure to control) often provide an alternative path to recovery.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;My dog&rsquo;s breed means I&rsquo;m automatically covered / excluded.&rdquo;</h2><p>Breed matters for insurance exclusions, not legal liability. Some homeowners policies exclude specific breeds (pit bulls, Rottweilers). If the policy excludes your dog&rsquo;s breed, coverage is denied and you must sue the owner personally. Legal liability under state statute applies regardless of breed.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I was teasing the dog a little, so I have no claim.&rdquo;</h2><p>Provocation is a defense, but it must be significant to bar recovery. Courts apply a reasonable person standard &mdash; minor irritation of a dog is not legally &ldquo;provocation.&rdquo; Even with some provocation, comparative fault rules in most states mean you may still recover a reduced amount.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I have to sue within a year.&rdquo;</h2><p>Most states have 2&ndash;3 year statutes of limitations for personal injury claims, including dog bites. Check your state&rsquo;s specific deadline. Claims involving government entities or government employees (police dogs) may have shorter deadlines and notice requirements.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I can only recover medical bills.&rdquo;</h2><p>Medical costs are the baseline, but dog bite plaintiffs routinely recover pain and suffering, emotional distress (especially for attack trauma), lost wages, future medical costs (plastic surgery, physical therapy), and permanent disfigurement damages. These non-economic damages often exceed the direct medical bills.</p>
